{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Submitted the fixed experiment that measures the automatic cadence, the last open point in the asserted defect's consequence, with previous_id db0da211f2d44ced97ecf68607d13d9d-experiment and the reason recorded (the previous execution called takeSnapshot directly and could not speak to cadence). The harness shortens SnapshotInterval to 100ms and, after driving a real single-node cluster with TrailingLogs=0 to the emptied state, samples store last index, snapshot index, applied index, stored-snapshot count and shouldSnapshot every 200ms for ~1.2s while issuing no client command, so any count growth is attributable to the node's own snapshot loop. Native construction feedback produced a result that narrows my earlier wording: the count rose 1→2 by 201ms and →3 by 402ms, then stayed at 3 for four more samples out to 1207ms while should_snapshot remained true with snapshot_index and applied_index unchanged at 3 - so the automatic path does add redundant snapshots at the unchanged index, but it did not keep adding one per interval in that window, and the plateau is not explained by the run (candidate causes such as snapshot-store retention or a later takeSnapshot error logged by runSnapshots were not captured). The earlier limitation phrase 'schedules a snapshot at the unchanged index on each interval' is explicitly flagged as now in question and to be narrowed to what the retained receipt shows.\"}"}}
